  "narrative": "Interpretation convention: for direction -1 PTEN, SMARCA4, and CDKN2A mean-difference claims, a positive signed estimate means the loss group has a more negative raw dependency_composite, i.e. stronger composite dependency. For direction +1 TP53 and KEAP1 claims, a positive signed estimate means weaker composite dependency. For interactions, a positive signed estimate means the claimed contrast is larger in the indicated subgroup than in its complement. The decision threshold is an absolute 0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction. I accept a mean-difference claim only when the point estimate and 95% interval credibly exceed the threshold. I reject claims when the evidence does not support a threshold-exceeding effect, especially when the point estimate is below threshold or the interval includes zero; unresolved claims are those with suggestive positive estimates but intervals too wide or too low to establish scientific meaningfulness.\n\nCDKN2A. H187 is the cleanest restricted CDKN2A context: TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1-intact, MTAP-intact, Avana-library, adherent-culture samples. The signed estimate is only +0.092 (95% +0.017 to +0.167). Although the interval remains positive and its upper bound barely touches the threshold, the point estimate is well below the threshold and the lower bound is far below it. In the cleanest context, this does not support a threshold-exceeding CDKN2A-loss association, so I reject H187. H192 tests the same core-intact Avana/adherent setting without the MTAP-intact restriction: +0.116 (+0.044 to +0.188). This is also not threshold-supported and remains near the borderline, but I close it as unresolved rather than reject outright because several CDKN2A contexts cluster around the threshold. H142, CDKN2A loss in core-intact lower-aneuploidy samples, is +0.137 (+0.024 to +0.249), again below threshold at the point estimate with a wide interval; it remains unresolved and closed. H143, CDKN2A loss in core-intact high mutation-burden samples, is +0.083 (-0.044 to +0.211), crosses zero, and is rejected. Overall, the newer clean and technical-stratum analyses argue against a robust threshold-exceeding CDKN2A-loss association. CDKN2A remains at most borderline and should not be accepted as a meaningful predictor.\n\nPTEN. H191 tests PTEN loss in the previously supported core-intact Avana/adherent context restricted to lower aneuploidy. The estimate is +0.305 with a 95% interval of +0.151 to +0.458. This is above threshold and just clears the lower bound, but the margin is small, so I accept it as a restricted secondary PTEN association while requesting independent validation. H193 tests broad high-EMT PTEN loss: +0.268 with lower bound +0.091. The point is above threshold, but the interval is wide and not threshold-supporting, so it remains unresolved and closed. H194 tests core-intact high-EMT PTEN loss: +0.176 with lower bound +0.026, also unresolved and closed. These results support the interpretation that PTEN is best interpreted as a context-dependent secondary association, not a broad population-level effect.\n\nPTEN interactions. H164 tests whether adherent culture modifies the core-intact PTEN contrast: +0.075 (-0.118 to +0.267), crosses zero and is rejected. H176 tests whether adenocarcinoma subtype modifies the PTEN contrast among TP53/SMARCA4-intact samples: -0.032 (-0.247 to +0.183), also crosses zero and is rejected. There is no evidence that these contextual variables modify the PTEN contrast.\n\nCore predictors in the dominant technical setting. H188 tests SMARCA4 loss in Avana/adherent samples: +0.798 (+0.716 to +0.880). H189 tests TP53 loss: +0.824 (+0.748 to +0.900). H190 tests KEAP1 loss: +0.844 (+0.764 to +0.923). All are large, direction-consistent, and far above threshold. I accept all three and close them; the strongest associations are not confined to non-Avana or non-adherent technical settings.\n\nI request validation of H191 because it is the most consequential new PTEN claim: it is above threshold but only marginally so, and independent validation will determine whether the restricted lower-aneuploidy PTEN association survives adjustment for validation multiplicity. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations on a composite dependency endpoint and do not establish causal knockout biology or clinical generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Current conclusions after iteration 11 discovery evidence:\n1. Core predictors: SMARCA4 loss remains strongly associated with stronger composite dependency, while TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss remain strongly associated with weaker composite dependency. These now hold in the dominant Avana-library/adherent-culture setting (H188-H190) and were already accepted in many strata.\n2. PTEN: remains a secondary context-dependent association. H191 accepts PTEN loss among TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1-intact Avana/adherent lower-aneuploidy samples at +0.305 (lower +0.151), but the lower bound is near threshold. Validation requested for H191. Broad high-EMT PTEN H193 and core-intact high-EMT PTEN H194 remain unresolved. PTEN interactions with adherent culture and adenocarcinoma subtype are rejected.\n3. CDKN2A: cleanest restricted Avana/adherent MTAP-intact core-intact context H187 is only +0.092 and is rejected as threshold-supporting. H192 (no MTAP restriction) is +0.116 and unresolved but closed; low-aneuploidy H142 is +0.137 and unresolved but closed; high-TMB H143 crosses zero and is rejected. The accumulated CDKN2A evidence does not support a robust threshold-exceeding association. Do not accept CDKN2A without a new robust interval above 0.15; prioritize H191 validation over further CDKN2A exploration.\n4. Pending validation: H191 is active pending independent validation. Synthesize validation before making final PTEN secondary claims; validation may downgrade H191 if adjusted lower bound falls below 0.15.\n5. General cautions: Discovery intervals are unadjusted; near-threshold claims need independent validation. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal effects or clinically generalizable biology.",
